AT Protocol means Authenticated Transfer Protocol.
It is a decentralized protocol for social web applications.
The [overview](https://atproto.com/guides/overview) separates account identity, data hosting, and application views.

## A DID identifies the account

A decentralized identifier is a persistent account identifier in AT Protocol, abbreviated DID.
It supports the `did:plc` and `did:web` methods.
The [DID specification](https://atproto.com/specs/did) defines their use and the account's DID document.

The [overview](https://atproto.com/guides/overview) distinguishes the DID from the human-readable handle.
The identity includes cryptographic keys and a reference to the current hosting provider.

## A PDS hosts the repository

A PDS is a Personal Data Server.
It hosts account data, manages identity, and routes requests to other services, as the [overview](https://atproto.com/guides/overview) describes.
The [repository specification](https://atproto.com/specs/repository) makes the account's PDS the authoritative repository host.
The DID document declares its location.

A repository holds the account's public records.
Its published versions have cryptographically signed commits.
The [repository specification](https://atproto.com/specs/repository) describes a content-addressed Merkle tree that supports verification of records and the complete repository.

Repository paths have two parts: a collection identifier and a record key.
The [path rules](https://atproto.com/specs/repository#repository-paths) define the collection as a namespaced identifier, or NSID.

## Lexicons describe records and calls

A lexicon is a [schema](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary) definition for records, HTTP endpoints, or event stream messages.
Lexicons are JSON files identified by NSIDs.
The [Lexicon specification](https://atproto.com/specs/lexicon) distinguishes record definitions from queries, procedures, and subscriptions.

A procedure schema describes an operation. It does not make that operation's input a public repository record.

The [overview](https://atproto.com/guides/overview) shows how DIDs, repositories, and lexicons fit together:

```text
DID document ── identifies keys and the current PDS
                                      │
                                      ▼
                            signed public repository
                                      │
                                      ▼
                         records described by lexicons
```

The [repository specification](https://atproto.com/specs/repository) makes repository contents public and verifiable.
A repository signature establishes verifiability. It does not hide the record's contents.

## Read Rat King next

[Rat King](/lore/rat-king) teaches how the agent-mail project uses these building blocks.
It distinguishes public profile records, private runtime bindings, and mailbox procedures.
Its page also separates implemented mail clients from the public-repository roadmap.
